hey what's happening people of the  internets it's dr john dolone here with  ramsay network today we are talking  about what do you do  when a parent who left you high and dry  reaches out and wants to reconcile so i  got an email to ask john ramsey  solutions.com  i'm going to read it to you today here  we go  dr d what's up what's up rolling in my  5.0 with my rack top down so my hair can  blow all that  hope you're doing well i am you too i'm  writing  to you to get some advice on my fiance's  lack of relationship with her mother  due to poor decisions made by her mother  when my fiance was young  her mother was removed from her life and  her dad raised her quite  excellently excellently is not a word  but i appreciate your  you're going for it there quite  excellently for a single dad they hadn't  had communication for nearly a decade  and recently she's been reaching out to  her on social media to try to reconnect  and she even suggested they meet for  lunch sometime  i've always known about her childhood  and i assume this would happen  eventually  i just thought or really hoped it would  be later in life we just graduated  college and are starting new jobs in  separate towns about two hours apart  while they're planning our wedding  that is in six months our relationship  is healthy  and we're going through some premarital  counseling i want to encourage  reconciliation but at the same time  i could also see negative effects coming  from that you're right  and you're right i love my fiance and  i'll work through anything relationally  but i'm not sure  how to start talking through this with  her awesome great question  good for you for being a fiance that  cares about his future relationship and  more importantly  cares about his future wife way to go  dude you get a gold dude star so now  that we have the internets  parents who were separated from their  kids whether it's because of adoption or  whether it's because  they had addiction issues and courts  took them away or whether there was  abuse  sexual abuse physical abuse emotional  abuse there was separation  man now you can just google find  facebook's find the interwebs and people  are tracking each other down  so if you've been separated from your  birth parents this is gonna happen to  you  more than likely this video is for folks  whose parents were addicts when they  were young and now they're clean  this video is for the dad who cheated on  mom  and he took off with the secretary and  now he's back after 10 years or 15 years  or 20 years  this video is for folks whose parents  were religious zealots  and now they've changed their hearts and  minds and they want to have  relationships this video is for folks  whose parents were  uber angry or super avoidant or  were hyper critical and now they  desperately want a relationship  what do you do when you get that first  facebook post or that instagram post  that says hey  this is your mom i'd love to talk so  here are four things you've got to  consider when you are thinking about  reconciliation number one  you got to let the fantasy go  you're not going to have a great  conversation and everyone's going to  head off to chuck e cheese that's not  how these  things go you're not going to get back  old birthday parties you're not going to  get back  learning how to ride a bicycle with your  dad in the cul-de-sac at the end of your  street and firework shows and all of  that  that stuff is gone a parent's job  a parent's job is to teach their kids  how to connect  how to love and be loved well and for  whatever reason  if you're watching this video your  parents did not do that this person  wasn't your mom this wasn't your dad  they were maybe your mother or your  father right they gave birth to you  but they didn't raise you and so you  have to know that in many ways you are  meeting a stranger  so you've got to let the fantasy go  number two be clear about why you want  to meet  are you just curious do you want to know  about your heritage  have you actually forgiven your birth  parent and you just want to circle up  and say hey  we're adults and i want to build a  relationship going this way  or do you want to show up and just ready  to unload  like a like a scene from some dramatized  lifetime movie  where you say the good thing and they  cry and then you hug at the end  none of that's gonna happen okay so you  have to ask yourself  why if you're going to a reconciliation  conversation  or you choose to meet your mom at  mcdonald's and your only reason to go is  to fight and yell  and show them how angry you are and tell  them the things  you're going to leave angry you're going  to leave hurt you're going to leave  empty  so ask yourself why do you want to have  this meeting why do you want to  reconcile why do you want to  come back together number three if you  choose to go ahead and meet in person  if you choose to be in contact  electronically or to make a phone call  you choose the boundaries and the terms  period you choose when and where the  meeting is going to happen  you choose when and where you're going  to communicate and how you're going to  communicate  you decide if this person is going to  get your cell number or your home  address  if they're going to meet your kids if  they're going to find out you have a dog  if they're going to know where you work  all of that  is on your terms the temptation when  you're running to see  an old parent an old birth mother an old  birth father  is to say oh my gosh and you dump right  back into those old rolls  and sometimes that can be manipulative  and controlling  one piece of advice never loan money  never tell them where you work and be  careful about telling people where you  live until you've established trust  and number four this is going to be  unpopular with a number of my mental  health  professional friends okay but i am a  huge fan of reconciliation  a step before reconciliation is this  idea of forgiveness  here's the deal you've got to forgive  you've all heard the story that  choosing not to forgive is like  poisoning yourself hoping somebody else  will die  choosing not to forgive is picking up a  giant cinder block of pain and just  voluntarily deciding i'm going to carry  this around  with me forever just because  now forgiveness doesn't mean you've got  to let somebody back in your life  forgiveness doesn't mean that you stick  your hand back in  to a bag where a rattlesnake is and let  it bite you again  that's just dumb right so reconciliation  happens when you're safe  and when you know where you're headed  moving forward when you are safe  when you have a good community at home  when you've got people in your life you  can be vulnerable  and whole with if you have a counselor  or a pastor or a spouse  or a fiance that you trust  reconciliation can be a powerful healing  moment  you're not going to ever clear up the  past but you can decide  to establish a new relationship going  forward  and it will be slow and it will be  intentional  and it will be painful  all of those things are the price of  admission for love  all of those things are the price of  admission for wholeness  and future wellness so where you can  i strongly encourage reconciliation but  you got to have a group of people with  you  you've got to be safe and you have to  make sure that those things happen on  your terms  so that's it for the video on  reconciling with an estranged mom  or dad if you're safe go for it  keep sending your emails to ask john  ramsey solutions.com  follow buttons go watch some other funny  youtube clips or some great  stand-up comedians probably somewhere in  the tubes and you can probably learn how  to fix your car  whatever's going on in the youtubes dr  john deloney ramsay network i love you  stay in school don't do drugs see you  soon

